  {
   "id": "r-two-values",
   "difficulty": 2,
   "skills": [
    "de-second"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "second-order"
   ],
   "prose": "Each model below is fitted to market data.",
   "question": "Which one needs two starting values?",
   "widget": {
    "kind": "choice",
    "layout": "stack",
    "options": [
     {
      "id": "ok",
      "text": "$x'' = -4x$",
      "correct": true
     },
     {
      "id": "first",
      "text": "$x' = -4x$",
      "why": "First order: one starting value fixes it."
     },
     {
      "id": "rate",
      "text": "$r' = 0.5(0.04 - r)$",
      "why": "The Vasicek drift is first order."
     },
     {
      "id": "log",
      "text": "$P' = 0.8P(1 - P/K)$",
      "why": "The logistic is first order."
     }
    ],
    "shuffle": true
   },
   "feedback": {
    "correct": "Second order needs $x(0)$ and $x'(0)$.",
    "generic": "Not quite right. Find the second derivative."
   },
   "explain": {
    "text": "$x'' = -4x$ has solutions $c_1\\cos 2t + c_2\\sin 2t$: two constants, two starting values."
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "r-roots",
   "difficulty": 2,
   "skills": [
    "de-second"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "char-roots"
   ],
   "prose": "Take $y'' - y' - 6y = 0$.",
   "question": "Which is the general solution?",
   "widget": {
    "kind": "choice",
    "layout": "stack",
    "options": [
     {
      "id": "ok",
      "text": "$c_1e^{3t} + c_2e^{-2t}$",
      "correct": true
     },
     {
      "id": "sign",
      "text": "$c_1e^{-3t} + c_2e^{2t}$",
      "why": "You flipped both signs. $r^2 - r - 6 = (r - 3)(r + 2)$."
     },
     {
      "id": "one",
      "text": "$c_1e^{3t}$",
      "why": "Two roots give two independent solutions."
     },
     {
      "id": "trig",
      "text": "$c_1\\cos 3t + c_2\\sin 2t$",
      "why": "The roots are real, so no sines."
     }
    ],
    "shuffle": true
   },
   "feedback": {
    "correct": "$(r - 3)(r + 2) = 0$: roots 3 and $-2$.",
    "generic": "Not quite right. Factor $r^2 - r - 6$."
   },
   "explain": {
    "text": "Roots 3 and $-2$ give $c_1e^{3t} + c_2e^{-2t}$; the $e^{3t}$ part grows unless $c_1 = 0$."
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "r-complex",
   "difficulty": 3,
   "skills": [
    "de-second"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "char-roots"
   ],
   "prose": "Take $y'' + 2y' + 2y = 0$.",
   "question": "Enter the characteristic root with positive imaginary part.",
   "widget": {
    "kind": "expression",
    "answer": "-1+i",
    "accept": [
     "-1+i",
     "i-1"
    ],
    "wrong": [
     {
      "id": "conj",
      "tex": "-1-i"
     },
     {
      "id": "sign",
      "tex": "1+i"
     }
    ],
    "keyboard": "algebra"
   },
   "feedback": {
    "correct": "$r = \\dfrac{-2 \\pm \\sqrt{4 - 8}}{2} = -1 \\pm i$.",
    "generic": "Not quite right. $b^2 - 4ac = -4$, and $\\sqrt{-4} = 2i$.",
    "byKey": {
     "conj": "That is the conjugate. The question asks for positive imaginary part.",
     "sign": "The real part is $-b/2 = -1$."
    }
   },
   "explain": {
    "text": "$-1 \\pm i$: solutions $e^{-t}(c_1\\cos t + c_2\\sin t)$."
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "r-ramp",
   "difficulty": 3,
   "skills": [
    "de-second"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "undetermined-coefficients"
   ],
   "prose": "Take $y'' + y' - 2y = 4t$, with the guess $y_p = at + b$.",
   "question": "What is $b$?",
   "widget": {
    "kind": "numeric",
    "answer": -1,
    "tol": {
     "abs": 0.001
    },
    "accept": "$-1$: $a = -2$, then $a - 2b = 0$",
    "wrong": [
     {
      "id": "a",
      "value": -2,
      "tol": {
       "abs": 0.001
      }
     },
     {
      "id": "plus",
      "value": 1,
      "tol": {
       "abs": 0.001
      }
     }
    ]
   },
   "feedback": {
    "correct": "$-2a = 4$ gives $a = -2$; $a - 2b = 0$ gives $b = -1$.",
    "generic": "Not quite right. Match the $t$ terms, then the constants.",
    "byKey": {
     "a": "That is $a$. The constants give $a - 2b = 0$, so $b = -1$.",
     "plus": "Check the sign: $-2 - 2b = 0$ gives $b = -1$."
    }
   },
   "explain": {
    "text": "$y_p = -2t - 1$: $y_p' = -2$, and $-2 - 2(-2t - 1) = 4t$."
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "r-resonant-form",
   "difficulty": 3,
   "skills": [
    "de-second"
   ],
   "tags": [
    "undetermined-coefficients"
   ],
   "prose": "Take $y'' + 9y = \\sin 3t$.",
   "question": "Which guess for $y_p$ works?",
   "widget": {
    "kind": "choice",
    "layout": "stack",
    "options": [
     {
      "id": "ok",
      "text": "$t(A\\cos 3t + B\\sin 3t)$",
      "correct": true
     },
     {
      "id": "plain",
      "text": "$A\\cos 3t + B\\sin 3t$",
      "why": "$\\sin 3t$ solves $y'' + 9y = 0$, so this guess gives 0."
     },
     {
      "id": "sin",
      "text": "$B\\sin 3t$",
      "why": "Same problem, and it drops the cosine."
     },
     {
      "id": "exp",
      "text": "$Ae^{3t}$",
      "why": "The forcing is a sine."
     }
    ],
    "shuffle": true
   },
   "feedback": {
    "correct": "Forcing at the natural frequency: multiply the guess by $t$.",
    "generic": "Not quite right. Check whether $\\sin 3t$ solves the homogeneous equation."
   },
   "explain": {
    "text": "The roots are $\\pm 3i$, so $\\sin 3t$ is homogeneous. The guess needs a factor $t$: resonance, $y_p = -\\tfrac{t}{6}\\cos 3t$."
   }
  },
